.TH DELIVER 1 "October 2026" "deliver 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
deliver \- deterministic deliverable validator for agent workflows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B deliver
[\fB--spec\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB--json\fR \fIJSON\fR]
[\fB--file\fR \fIPATTERN\fR...]
[\fB--base\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB--format\fR \fItext|json|sarif|junit|ndjson|github\fR]
[\fB--color\fR \fIauto|always|never\fR]
[\fB--progress\fR \fIauto|always|never\fR]
[\fB--strict\fR]
[\fB--allow-commands\fR]
[\fB--jobs\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB--fail-fast\fR]
[\fB--incremental\fR]
[\fB--cache-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB--attest\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fB--attest-key\fR \fIPATH\fR]]
.br
.B deliver
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B deliver
validates concrete task outputs from a TOML or JSON spec. It can check file
existence, size limits, required and forbidden regexes, hashes, schemas,
built-in secret patterns, directory shape, SBOM presence and shape,
subprocess-plugin gates, command exit status, stdout and stderr content, and
Git cleanliness \- and can write and verify signed attestation bundles for
passing runs.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init [--output \fIPATH\fR]
Write a starter spec file to \fIPATH\fR (default \fBdeliver.toml\fR). Fails
if the file already exists.
.TP
.B validate \fIPATH\fR
Parse the spec at \fIPATH\fR (TOML or JSON, by extension) and report a syntax
error without running any checks.
.TP
.B completions \fISHELL\fR
Print shell completion script source for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, or
\fBfish\fR to stdout.
.TP
.B schema
Print the spec format as a JSON Schema (draft-07) document to stdout, for
editor and agent tooling to validate or autocomplete a spec file.
.TP
.B keygen [--out \fIPREFIX\fR]
Generate an Ed25519 keypair for \fB--attest\fR signing: writes
\fIPREFIX\fR.key (keep private; mode 0600) and \fIPREFIX\fR.pub. Default
prefix \fBdeliver\fR.
.TP
.B attest verify \fIPATH\fR [--pub \fIPATH\fR]
Verify an attestation bundle: payload integrity always, and the signature
against the supplied public key when \fB--pub\fR is given (otherwise only
self-consistency with the embedded key). Exits 1 on any failure.
.SH SPEC COMPOSITION
A spec file may set a top-level \fBextends\fR key naming a parent spec,
resolved relative to the directory of the spec that declares it. Checks
accumulate (parent checks run first); Git-cleanliness flags combine with
\fBOR\fR, so a child spec can only add requirements on top of its parent.
Circular \fBextends\fR chains are rejected with an error.
.SH CONDITIONAL CHECKS
Any \fB[[file]]\fR, \fB[[command]]\fR, or \fB[[directory]]\fR check may set a
\fBwhen\fR table of skip conditions: \fBfile_exists\fR (a base-relative path
that must exist) and/or \fBos\fR (\fBlinux\fR, \fBmacos\fR, or \fBwindows\fR,
case-insensitive). Conditions combine with AND. When a condition does not
hold, the check is skipped rather than failed: skipped checks report
\fBpass = true\fR with a \fBskipped\fR flag in JSON, a \fB<skipped/>\fR
element in JUnit XML, are excluded from weighted completion, and never fail a
run. A \fBfile_exists\fR path that would escape \fB--base\fR never matches.
.SH SUPPLY-CHAIN CHECKS
File checks accept \fBforbid_secrets\fR: fail when the file matches a
built-in, high-confidence secret pattern (AWS keys, provider tokens, private
key blocks, JWTs, assigned credentials). Reports name the pattern and line,
never the matched content.
.PP
A spec may declare one \fB[sbom]\fR section: \fBrequired\fR (default true)
fails when no SBOM exists under \fB--base\fR, and \fBformats\fR restricts
accepted documents to \fBspdx\fR or \fBcyclonedx\fR. Searched locations:
\fB*.spdx\fR, \fB*.spdx.json\fR, \fB*.cdx.json\fR, \fBbom.json\fR.
.SH PLUGIN CHECKS
\fB[[plugin]]\fR checks extend \fBdeliver\fR with organization-specific
gates: the runner spawns the plugin, writes one JSON request line to its
stdin ({"protocol":1,"name":...,"base":...}), closes stdin, and expects one
JSON response object with exit 0: {"pass":true|false,"message":"..."}. A
nonzero exit, unparseable stdout, a missing \fBpass\fR boolean, or output
over 1 MiB per stream fails the check with the plugin's stderr quoted.
Plugins are arbitrary code and require \fB--allow-commands\fR like command
checks; there is no sandboxing. They support \fBcwd\fR, \fBtimeout_secs\fR
(process-group kill on timeout, Unix), \fBweight\fR, \fBwhen\fR, and
accumulate across \fBextends\fR. See examples/plugin/ for a runnable
example.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -s, --spec \fIPATH\fR
Read a TOML or JSON validation spec from \fIPATH\fR.
.TP
.B --json \fIJSON\fR
Read a JSON validation spec from the command line. This overrides
\fB--spec\fR.
.TP
.B --file \fIPATTERN\fR...
Run quick required-file checks. Simple \fB*\fR and \fB?\fR glob patterns are
expanded recursively below \fB--base\fR.
.TP
.B -b, --base \fIDIR\fR
Resolve relative file paths and command working directories from \fIDIR\fR.
Defaults to the current directory. Spec-selected paths may not escape this
directory, including through symlinks.
.TP
.B --allow-commands
Run command and plugin checks after reviewing the spec and its full extends
chain. Commands and plugins inherit the runner's privileges and environment.
Without this flag, specs containing them fail before any checks run. This is
not a sandbox; isolate untrusted repositories in CI.
.TP
.B -f, --format \fItext|json|sarif|junit|ndjson|github\fR
Select the report format. Text is for people; JSON is for generic tools;
\fBsarif\fR emits SARIF 2.1.0 (failed checks only, for GitHub code scanning
and similar); \fBjunit\fR emits JUnit XML (every check, for CI dashboards);
\fBndjson\fR streams one JSON line per check the moment it finishes, then a
summary line, so watchers see partial progress; \fBgithub\fR emits GitHub
Actions workflow commands (failing checks as ::error annotations plus a
::notice summary).
.TP
.B -j, --jobs \fIN\fR
Run file, command, and directory checks across up to \fIN\fR worker threads;
Git checks always run last and sequentially. Default \fB1\fR (sequential).
\fB0\fR uses all available CPUs. Report order always matches spec order
regardless of \fIN\fR.
.TP
.B --fail-fast
Stop running remaining checks after the first failure. Skipped checks are
reported as skipped, never as passed, and the run still fails overall. With
\fB--jobs\fR > 1 the set of checks that still runs is timing-dependent.
.TP
.B --incremental
Re-check only files changed since \fBHEAD\fR, replaying cached results for
unchanged files. Requires \fB--base\fR inside a Git repository with at least
one commit, and implies the default \fB--cache-dir\fR when none is given.
Applies to \fB--spec\fR and \fB--json\fR runs.
.TP
.B --cache-dir \fIDIR\fR
Directory for the result cache (one JSON file per \fB--base\fR). Replays
file-check results for identical file content; entries are keyed by the
deliver version plus the full check definition, so any spec edit invalidates
them. Replayed results are marked \fB(cached)\fR. Cache failures are never
fatal.
.TP
.B --color \fIauto|always|never\fR
Control ANSI color in text output. The default, \fBauto\fR, enables color only
when stdout is a terminal.
.TP
.B --progress \fIauto|always|never\fR
Control the stderr progress spinner. The default, \fBauto\fR, shows progress
for interactive text runs.
.TP
.B --strict
Exit with status 1 when any validation check fails.
.TP
.B --attest \fIPATH\fR
Write an attestation bundle to PATH after a passing run: spec and report
hashes, tallies, the git commit when available, a timestamp, and an Ed25519
signature when \fB--attest-key\fR is supplied. Runs that do not pass never
produce a bundle. Status is printed to stderr.
.TP
.B --attest-key \fIPATH\fR
Ed25519 private key file (from \fBdeliver keygen\fR) used to sign the
\fB--attest\fR bundle.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Validation completed. Checks may have failed unless \fB--strict\fR was used.
.TP
.B 1
At least one validation check failed and \fB--strict\fR was set.
.TP
.B 2
The command could not read or parse its inputs, or no checks were provided.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B deliver --spec deliver.toml --allow-commands --strict
Run the project validation spec and fail the process on any failed check.
.TP
.B deliver --file README.md src/*.rs --color always
Check files directly and force colored text output.
.TP
.B deliver --spec deliver.toml --allow-commands --format json
Emit a stable JSON report for another tool.
.TP
.B deliver init && deliver validate deliver.toml
Scaffold a spec and confirm it parses before running it.
